---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Nav from '../../components/Nav.astro';
import Footer from '../../components/Footer.astro';

// The text below is NOT authored here. Its source of truth is caliper/docs/privacy-policy.md in
// the Caliper repo, where it lives next to the code it describes, so a change to what the app does
// and a change to what this page claims can land in one commit. Every factual claim was checked
// against Caliper 1.0.0 on 2026-09-18 — including, specifically:
//
//   * packaging/Caliper.entitlements contains app-sandbox, device.audio-input and
//     files.user-selected.read-write, and NOTHING else. There is no network.client.
//   * The binary carries no WebBrowserComponent symbols and no WKWebView strings
//     (JUCE_WEB_BROWSER=0). WebKit shows in `otool -L` because JUCE's module system links the
//     framework for juce_gui_extra regardless; nothing in Caliper can instantiate a web view.
//
// THE NETWORK CLAIM IS LOAD-BEARING AND IS TRUE BECAUSE OF THE ENTITLEMENTS FILE, not as a policy
// choice. If a future version ever adds network.client — for a corpus upload, a licence check,
// anything — this page and the source file must change in the SAME commit, or this becomes a false
// claim in an App Store listing. Do not soften it and do not broaden it.
//
// This page is an App Store Connect requirement for Caliper (com.warrenlabs.caliper), on both the
// Mac and iOS listings. It must stay reachable anonymously, with no password gate.
//
// NOT REVIEWED BY A LAWYER. It describes the app's behaviour accurately. Whether that is
// *sufficient* for the GDPR, the CCPA or Montana law is open, and the source file says so too.
const updated = '18 September 2026';
---

<Layout
  title="Privacy · Caliper · Warren Labs"
  description="How Caliper handles your data. Nothing is sent unless you press Submit: no account, no analytics, no telemetry, and no background upload."
>
  <Nav variant="home" />
  <section class="sec">
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <span class="eyebrow">Caliper</span>
      <h2>Privacy</h2>
      <p class="byline">
        <b>Caliper</b>, by Warren Labs. Last updated {updated}.
      </p>

      <p class="sec-lede">
        Caliper sends nothing unless you press Submit. Your measurements are files on your own
        device. There is no account, no analytics, no telemetry, and nothing is uploaded in the
        background.
      </p>

      <h3>What changed, and when</h3>
      <p>
        <b>Until 18 September 2026 this page said Caliper requested no network capability at all</b>,
        and leaned on that as a checkable fact rather than a promise. It now has one, so that you
        can contribute a measurement from inside the app instead of exporting a file, opening a
        browser, finding the file and uploading it by hand. The capability and this page changed in
        the same commit, which is the rule the policy sets for itself.
      </p>

      <h3>What is still true, and still checkable</h3>
      <ul class="points">
        <li>
          <b>The network is used for exactly one thing: an upload you start.</b> There is no other
          outbound request in the app — no analytics library, no crash reporter, no telemetry, no
          licence check, no update check, no remote configuration.
        </li>
        <li>
          <b>Nothing is sent automatically, ever.</b> Measuring, saving, exporting and verifying are
          entirely local. A measurement leaves your device only when you choose
          <b>Share to Warren Labs</b> and confirm what is being sent.
        </li>
        <li>
          <b>The interface is native, not a web view.</b> There is no embedded browser that could
          fetch anything on its own.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h3>What a submission sends, when you make one</h3>
      <p>Only when you choose <b>Share to Warren Labs</b>, and only what the bundle contains:</p>
      <ul class="points">
        <li>The measured curves, and the impulse responses they were computed from</li>
        <li>The rig, fixture and calibration serials, the analysis chain, and the tier the run earned</li>
        <li>The metadata you typed — brand, model, unit, pads, jig, amp</li>
        <li>The name or handle you give the submission</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        <b>It sends nothing from your device that is not in that bundle.</b> No contacts, no
        identifiers, no location, no list of your other files. The app shows you the summary before
        it sends, and the bundle is the same one Save Session writes — you can open it first.
      </p>
      <p>
        A submitted measurement is published under a non-commercial licence, with attribution, and
        you keep your copyright.
      </p>

      <h3>What the app has access to, and why</h3>
      <p>
        <b>The microphone.</b> This is Caliper's purpose: it records the measurement capsules on
        your acoustic fixture. Audio is analysed on your device and written to files you choose.
      </p>
      <p>
        <b>Files you pick.</b> Calibration files you open, and the folder you save a measurement
        into. Only what you select in a system panel.
      </p>
      <p>
        <b>Its own container.</b> App settings, and a copy of each calibration file — so a stored
        measurement cannot be invalidated later by the original being edited underneath it.
      </p>
      <p>Nothing here leaves the device.</p>

      <h3>What Caliper does not do</h3>
      <ul class="points">
        <li>No account, no sign-in, no email address.</li>
        <li>No advertising, no tracking, no third-party SDKs.</li>
        <li>No collection of usage data, device identifiers, or location.</li>
        <li>
          No automatic upload of measurements, and no background sync. <b>If you submit a
          measurement, you do it deliberately</b> — you press Submit, and you are shown what is
          being sent first.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Sharing is yours, and it ends our involvement</h3>
      <p>
        Caliper can export a measurement bundle or a graphic. Once you hand that to another app, a
        message, or a website, that destination's privacy policy governs it and ours no longer
        applies. We never see it and have no way to.
      </p>

      <h3>Children</h3>
      <p>
        Caliper is a measurement instrument for audio work. It is not directed at children, and it
        collects no data from anyone, of any age.
      </p>

      <h3>Changes</h3>
      <p>
        Material changes will be reflected here with a new date. Because the app collects nothing,
        the most likely change is a new capability — and if one ever transmits anything, it will say
        so plainly here <i>before</i> it ships, not after.
      </p>

      <h3>Contact</h3>
      <p><a href="mailto:jamey@warrenlabs.com">jamey@warrenlabs.com</a></p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <Footer />
</Layout>
